findings.what:
V5 says build, 'given a milestone, adds one model per window view of gen_milestone(M)', with its diagram at gen/<gm>/diagram/<stem>.json. gen_milestone maps every milestone that is not M<k> to M2. So every test-milestone build is 'given a milestone' whose window view is M2's m1_view, and every copy_repo() copy contains that view. These builds are:
- holon.rules._given_par_inputs (PAR derivation of T24, T25, ...: milestone T.., diagrams in memory, adopted=False);
- holon.codecrun.test_evidence_set (the same, to reproduce DERIVATION.json's evidence_sha256);
- holon.rules.par_lossy.components with --codec;
- I24's instrument tests_m3/_i24.evidence_of (milestone 'T24', diagrams in memory, adopted left True), used by tests_m3/test_derive_par.py and test_par_outcomes.py.
These builds pass diagrams= for their own documents only, which leaves two outcomes. If the view's diagram is taken from that argument, as fact documents' diagrams are, build raises 'no diagram was passed in', and these derivations and the I24 instrument fail. If it is read from the committed file, every test-milestone evidence set gains M2's view model and its atoms. Test-milestone derivations then depend on gen/M2/extracted/m1_view.json, gen/M2/diagram/m1_view.json and spec/M2/lib/window_rollup.json.
'As it adds the adopted documents' may mean the adopted switch: I24 passes adopted=False to keep M2's adopted documents out of PAR sets. V5 does not say so, and the I24 instrument does not pass adopted=False. R4 checks only an M2 build and a build for no milestone, and V8 lists no I24 test.

Each of P1-P4 follows from committed inputs once V2-V5 are implemented.
The removed requires are commitBatch[21], commitBatchWithProof[25,30], commitState[11,20], finalizeBatch[14], proveState[9] and setPause[1,2]. They hold no call. Their state reads are qlen, D, t, qpend and paused; everything else they read is a let or an input. A let named in a require adds no access: access.Walker.read returns for a bound local, and the let's own guard already read its expression.
So a removed require changes only its own TRANS edge's reads, and only of unkeyed scalars. It never changes a write, an edge, a keyed carrier's imprecision, a principal class (the classes key on reasons 1, 23 and 28, none of which is removed) or an E4 position (the recomputations at commitBatch guards[15].expr and commitBatchWithProof guards[11].expr come before every removed index). The diagram's TRANS guards are index-aligned with the facts' guards. challengeState writes bc, chs, inch and win (and the log), and no removed require reads any of them.
The predicted values follow:
- P1: the 62 edges, writes, reads and guard lists;
- P2: the unchanged models, atoms, header and inputs;
- P3: the 35 E2 atoms with the same via, the twelve unchanged endpoints, and the probe's [E2, E6] (the abstraction's 35 relevant atoms are 32 E2 + 2 E6 + 1 E5, and V5 removes E5 by design);
- P4: E6, E4 and the E3 re-indexing (8, computed with the instrument).
The same reading rules out the risks the prompt still lists for P3 ('a removed require is the only read of a carrier', 'imprecision differs'). P3's derived-law clause accepts named [] or [TRANS.challengeState] (test line 427), so it cannot fail on that point. Only an error in the build's own V2-V5 code can make any of P1-P4 fail.
